WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.980 --> 00:00:07.670
Hi, I'm Jamie.

00:00:07.670 --> 00:00:09.340
And I'm going to
talk about developing

00:00:09.340 --> 00:00:11.470
internal tools for
multilingual teams,

00:00:11.470 --> 00:00:15.310
specifically teams
that use Python and R.

00:00:15.310 --> 00:00:16.400
To get started,

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:18.317
I want to talk about pizza.

00:00:18.317 --> 00:00:20.650
Imagine you having a couple
of friends over for dinner

00:00:20.650 --> 00:00:22.550
and you need to
prepare them something.

00:00:22.550 --> 00:00:25.670
The easiest thing to do is to
prepare them the same meal.

00:00:25.670 --> 00:00:27.970
However, they're both
pretty fussy eaters.

00:00:27.970 --> 00:00:30.100
Do you make two
entirely different meals

00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:31.520
with all the extra ingredients,

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:33.200
prep time and aggravation?

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:36.010
No, you make pizza
because even if you

00:00:36.010 --> 00:00:37.840
make a margherita
pizza for one friend

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:39.610
and a Hawaiian for
the other, they still

00:00:39.610 --> 00:00:43.510
have the same base, the
same core ingredients.

00:00:43.510 --> 00:00:46.660
What does pizza have
to do with R and Python?

00:00:46.660 --> 00:00:49.990
The prospect of developing
a tool in both R and Python

00:00:49.990 --> 00:00:51.640
can feel like you're
about to prepare

00:00:51.640 --> 00:00:53.460
two entirely different meals.

00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:56.890
It's twice the effort,
twice the cognitive load

00:00:56.890 --> 00:00:59.230
and can take a lot more time.

00:00:59.230 --> 00:01:01.570
How can we take the
principle of our pizza party,

00:01:01.570 --> 00:01:04.900
that is making two different
meals with the same base,

00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:08.710
and apply it to developing
bilingual tools?

00:01:08.710 --> 00:01:11.790
This is a question I think
about a lot in my job.

00:01:11.790 --> 00:01:13.110
So I work at Bumble.

00:01:13.110 --> 00:01:16.000
We are the company behind the
dating and social networking

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:18.520
apps, Badoo, Bumble and Fruitz.

00:01:18.520 --> 00:01:22.850
My job is to develop internal
packages for our data analysts.

00:01:22.850 --> 00:01:24.840
Now most of our
analysts work in Python.

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:27.710
However, some of our
projects are done in R.

00:01:27.710 --> 00:01:30.200
This means some of
our internal packages

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:33.430
are developed in both languages.

00:01:33.430 --> 00:01:35.800
But why do we need
internal packages?

00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:39.100
Well, internal packages can
help to automate and standardize

00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:42.520
procedures that are specific
to your organization.

00:01:42.520 --> 00:01:45.280
For example, an internal
package might automatically

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:47.830
apply company
styling to a report

00:01:47.830 --> 00:01:51.400
or enable a user to connect
to an internal database

00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:53.680
in the correct way.

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:54.440
To borrow from

00:01:54.440 --> 00:01:56.770
Emily Riederer's
RStudio global talk,

00:01:56.770 --> 00:01:58.930
internal packages are
kind of like members

00:01:58.930 --> 00:02:01.000
of your organization
with specific jobs

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:05.020
to do. But why develop in
both Python and R?

00:02:05.020 --> 00:02:06.340
The answer is simple.

00:02:06.340 --> 00:02:08.470
In a multilingual
organization, we

00:02:08.470 --> 00:02:11.830
want everyone to follow the
same internal procedures,

00:02:11.830 --> 00:02:15.623
regardless of the language
that they're using.

00:02:15.623 --> 00:02:17.290
The question I will
try to address today

00:02:17.290 --> 00:02:19.420
is, what strategies
can we use to make

00:02:19.420 --> 00:02:23.610
developing tools simultaneously
with Python and R easier?

00:02:23.610 --> 00:02:25.530
In truth, this is
a very big topic

00:02:25.530 --> 00:02:27.810
with lots of branching ideas.

00:02:27.810 --> 00:02:32.160
Therefore, today I will focus
on three ideas that have helped

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:36.200
me to develop bilingual tools.

00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:37.820
The first idea I
want to talk about

00:02:37.820 --> 00:02:40.219
is building identical
generic functions.

00:02:40.219 --> 00:02:41.900
What is a generic function?

00:02:41.900 --> 00:02:44.490
Let's look at this
from the R perspective.

00:02:44.490 --> 00:02:47.710
Generic functions are very
common in the R language.

00:02:47.710 --> 00:02:51.380
For example, functions like
print or summary or tidy

00:02:51.380 --> 00:02:52.730
from the broom package.

00:02:52.730 --> 00:02:54.590
The really cool
thing about generics

00:02:54.590 --> 00:02:56.450
is that they behave
in different ways

00:02:56.450 --> 00:02:59.420
depending on the class of an
object that is passed to them.

00:02:59.420 --> 00:03:02.360
Under the hood, this is
achieved using a process called

00:03:02.360 --> 00:03:04.790
method dispatch.

00:03:04.790 --> 00:03:06.800
As an internal
developer, you might

00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:10.010
want to build a package that
uses custom internal classes,

00:03:10.010 --> 00:03:11.930
and you may want
your users to be

00:03:11.930 --> 00:03:14.690
able to call methods like
print or summary on them.

00:03:14.690 --> 00:03:16.370
In some cases,
you may even want

00:03:16.370 --> 00:03:18.718
to define your own
generic functions.

00:03:18.718 --> 00:03:20.510
Let's take a look at
what generic functions

00:03:20.510 --> 00:03:22.280
look like in code.

00:03:22.280 --> 00:03:24.200
To do this, we'll
create a new package

00:03:24.200 --> 00:03:27.170
which will provide one
function, say hello,

00:03:27.170 --> 00:03:30.110
and this will print a nice,
friendly greeting to an object

00:03:30.110 --> 00:03:32.330
that we pass to it.

00:03:32.330 --> 00:03:34.830
To start, we define
our generic function,

00:03:34.830 --> 00:03:36.950
say hello, and in
the function body

00:03:36.950 --> 00:03:39.790
we make a call to use
method passing it.

00:03:39.790 --> 00:03:42.500
The name of our
generic as a string.

00:03:42.500 --> 00:03:43.290
And that's it.

00:03:43.290 --> 00:03:44.540
That's our generic.

00:03:44.540 --> 00:03:46.880
But now we need a class
specific implementation

00:03:46.880 --> 00:03:48.200
of this function.

00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:51.590
And to do this, let, let's
make one for a dataframe.

00:03:51.590 --> 00:03:53.690
And to do it we will
use dot notation,

00:03:53.690 --> 00:03:56.030
say hello dot dataframe.

00:03:56.030 --> 00:03:58.520
And in this new
function we print

00:03:58.520 --> 00:04:00.790
a nice, friendly greeting.

00:04:00.790 --> 00:04:03.910
And now if you want to catch
all implementation, say hello.

00:04:03.910 --> 00:04:05.780
We will use the
default sudo class,

00:04:05.780 --> 00:04:11.390
say hello dot default.
OK now we know how

00:04:11.390 --> 00:04:13.670
our R package is going to work.

00:04:13.670 --> 00:04:16.220
We need a Python version.

00:04:16.220 --> 00:04:20.660
Can we write our Python version
in the same way as our R vision?

00:04:20.660 --> 00:04:22.610
And the answer is Yes.

00:04:22.610 --> 00:04:25.220
To do this, we're going
to import single dispatch

00:04:25.220 --> 00:04:27.690
from the functools module.

00:04:27.690 --> 00:04:29.640
And unlike in R,
we actually start

00:04:29.640 --> 00:04:33.090
by defining our fallback
method for say hello.

00:04:33.090 --> 00:04:35.610
And we decorate it
with single dispatch.

00:04:35.610 --> 00:04:38.040
Now, if you're not
familiar with decorators,

00:04:38.040 --> 00:04:40.530
think of them as wrapper
functions that change

00:04:40.530 --> 00:04:43.500
the behavior of a function.

00:04:43.500 --> 00:04:46.110
And now we're going
to create a dataframe

00:04:46.110 --> 00:04:47.610
implementation of say hello.

00:04:47.610 --> 00:04:52.470
In Python using a dataframe
object from the pandas library.

00:04:52.470 --> 00:04:54.250
And we do this with
the register function.

00:04:54.250 --> 00:04:57.330
And here I am passing that
dataframe to the register

00:04:57.330 --> 00:05:00.780
function. And there we have it.

00:05:00.780 --> 00:05:03.370
But what have we
actually achieved here?

00:05:03.370 --> 00:05:07.420
Well, we have two different
tools, one in R one in Python,

00:05:07.420 --> 00:05:09.970
that have the same
foundation, a say hello

00:05:09.970 --> 00:05:13.710
generic with a data
frame implementation.

00:05:13.710 --> 00:05:15.850
So both of my packages
work the same

00:05:15.850 --> 00:05:19.030
conceptually. This is
good for end users.

00:05:19.030 --> 00:05:21.300
They get a similar API to
work with regardless of

00:05:21.300 --> 00:05:24.380
whether they're using
R or Python.

00:05:24.380 --> 00:05:28.110
But also my code is
physically quite similar.

00:05:28.110 --> 00:05:30.680
So when I go and make a
change in the Python version

00:05:30.680 --> 00:05:33.500
of my code, I know
where to go and make

00:05:33.500 --> 00:05:36.900
that change in the R
version of my code.

00:05:36.900 --> 00:05:39.750
So building identical
generic functions

00:05:39.750 --> 00:05:42.820
is going to save me a
lot of time and effort.

00:05:42.820 --> 00:05:46.050
The next idea I will talk
about is identical error

00:05:46.050 --> 00:05:48.690
handling with classes.

00:05:48.690 --> 00:05:50.970
Let's look at this from
the Python perspective.

00:05:50.970 --> 00:05:52.470
Now, if you're a
Python user, you're

00:05:52.470 --> 00:05:54.940
probably familiar with
these kinds of exceptions,

00:05:54.940 --> 00:05:58.890
file not found error, zero
division error, and key error.

00:05:58.890 --> 00:06:00.660
Exceptions in Python
are themselves

00:06:00.660 --> 00:06:03.450
classes that inherit from
other exception classes,

00:06:03.450 --> 00:06:04.825
and you can probably
already tell

00:06:04.825 --> 00:06:06.242
that the names of
these exceptions

00:06:06.242 --> 00:06:07.310
are quite descriptive.

00:06:07.310 --> 00:06:10.500
They form part of
the error message.

00:06:10.500 --> 00:06:12.210
Now, as an internal
developer, you

00:06:12.210 --> 00:06:15.040
can create your own
custom internal errors.

00:06:15.040 --> 00:06:17.070
You can give them really
descriptive names,

00:06:17.070 --> 00:06:19.020
and the error messages
that they give

00:06:19.020 --> 00:06:21.960
can be as well
specific as you want,

00:06:21.960 --> 00:06:25.950
even telling the user where to
go for help if that error has

00:06:25.950 --> 00:06:28.030
been thrown.

00:06:28.030 --> 00:06:30.400
The powerful thing about
exceptions in Python

00:06:30.400 --> 00:06:32.950
is that they can be handled
explicitly, for example,

00:06:32.950 --> 00:06:34.460
with an except statement.

00:06:34.460 --> 00:06:36.550
I asked Python to
try running some code

00:06:36.550 --> 00:06:39.430
and in the event of a
specific error, in this case

00:06:39.430 --> 00:06:41.710
a file not found
error, I can run

00:06:41.710 --> 00:06:43.390
an alternative block of code.

00:06:43.390 --> 00:06:46.000
This gives me maximum
control over how

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:47.160
my code handles failure.

00:06:50.140 --> 00:06:51.980
So we're building
a Python package

00:06:51.980 --> 00:06:55.990
and we are handling
errors in this way.

00:06:55.990 --> 00:07:00.689
But can we handle errors in
the same way in our R packages?

00:07:00.689 --> 00:07:03.750
The answer is yes, but let's
look at some basic error

00:07:03.750 --> 00:07:05.195
handling in R first.

00:07:08.470 --> 00:07:09.970
If you're an R
user, you may have

00:07:09.970 --> 00:07:14.020
seen one of these two functions,
stop from base R, and abort

00:07:14.020 --> 00:07:15.410
from the rlang package.

00:07:15.410 --> 00:07:18.760
Both functions signal
an error and in R,

00:07:18.760 --> 00:07:20.500
if we want to handle
errors in our code,

00:07:20.500 --> 00:07:23.440
we can use something like
a try catch function.

00:07:23.440 --> 00:07:25.780
But notice here on
the second line,

00:07:25.780 --> 00:07:29.620
the error handler is a function
that will execute in response

00:07:29.620 --> 00:07:31.120
to any error.

00:07:31.120 --> 00:07:33.670
It's not designed to
handle a specific error,

00:07:33.670 --> 00:07:34.970
and this is a problem.

00:07:34.970 --> 00:07:37.450
I now have a lot less
control over my code

00:07:37.450 --> 00:07:40.390
and more importantly, my
Python and R packages

00:07:40.390 --> 00:07:44.020
handle failure in
very different ways.

00:07:44.020 --> 00:07:46.940
How can we overcome this?

00:07:46.940 --> 00:07:51.470
Well, the answer to this is
within the abort function

00:07:51.470 --> 00:07:53.960
from rlang.

00:07:53.960 --> 00:07:55.620
Let me illustrate
this with an example.

00:07:55.620 --> 00:07:58.460
I'm going to create a new
function, abort credentials

00:07:58.460 --> 00:07:59.710
missing.

00:07:59.710 --> 00:08:02.170
And within the call
to rlang abort,

00:08:02.170 --> 00:08:05.900
I am going to give a
specific class name. Here,

00:08:05.900 --> 00:08:09.560
It's error credentials missing.

00:08:09.560 --> 00:08:13.520
Now the really great thing
about error functions in R

00:08:13.520 --> 00:08:18.950
is that you can create an
equivalent Python exception.

00:08:18.950 --> 00:08:23.060
So as an internal developer,
you have your Python exceptions

00:08:23.060 --> 00:08:24.878
and you have your
R error functions.

00:08:24.878 --> 00:08:25.920
They're one and the same.

00:08:25.920 --> 00:08:28.100
So when you make a
change in one language,

00:08:28.100 --> 00:08:30.980
you know where to make that
change in the other language.

00:08:30.980 --> 00:08:35.390
But what have we gained by
giving our error a class name?

00:08:35.390 --> 00:08:37.290
Well, let's go back
to try catch.

00:08:37.290 --> 00:08:38.990
You can see here
on the second line,

00:08:38.990 --> 00:08:42.830
I can now write a specific
error handler for my new error,

00:08:42.830 --> 00:08:47.540
error credentials missing and now
my Python package and my R package

00:08:47.540 --> 00:08:51.530
handle failure in the same way.

00:08:51.530 --> 00:08:54.110
I want to round off
this section on errors

00:08:54.110 --> 00:08:56.300
by talking about error chaining.

00:08:56.300 --> 00:08:59.870
In essence, error chaining is
throwing an error in response

00:08:59.870 --> 00:09:01.070
to another error.

00:09:01.070 --> 00:09:03.230
One use for this is
to give a high level

00:09:03.230 --> 00:09:06.800
context to a lower level error.

00:09:06.800 --> 00:09:09.080
Doing this in
Python is very easy.

00:09:09.080 --> 00:09:13.320
For example, you can give your
original error an alias. Here

00:09:13.320 --> 00:09:15.260
I'm giving it the
alias E. And then

00:09:15.260 --> 00:09:20.360
I'm raising another exception
from that original exception.

00:09:20.360 --> 00:09:22.610
And this gives me a kind
of double error message.

00:09:22.610 --> 00:09:24.830
I get my original error
and then Python tells me

00:09:24.830 --> 00:09:28.280
that this error caused
another error.

00:09:28.280 --> 00:09:30.390
Can we emulate this in R?

00:09:30.390 --> 00:09:31.797
The answer is yes.

00:09:31.797 --> 00:09:34.380
Now, the way that we're going
to do this is we're going to use

00:09:34.380 --> 00:09:39.040
try fetch from the rlang
package instead of a try catch.

00:09:39.040 --> 00:09:41.780
And in my error handler, I'm
going to pass my condition

00:09:41.780 --> 00:09:45.280
object to the abort
function, specifically

00:09:45.280 --> 00:09:46.320
the parent argument.

00:09:49.640 --> 00:09:53.060
And here I get a kind
of double error message.

00:09:53.060 --> 00:09:54.170
And then we have it.

00:09:54.170 --> 00:09:57.650
Our Python and R packages
now conduct error chaining

00:09:57.650 --> 00:09:59.120
in the same way.

00:09:59.120 --> 00:10:00.740
The third idea I
want to talk about

00:10:00.740 --> 00:10:03.530
is creating your own
internal wrappers.

00:10:03.530 --> 00:10:06.000
But what is an
internal wrapper?

00:10:06.000 --> 00:10:09.150
Well, it's a package
written in one language that

00:10:09.150 --> 00:10:11.790
wraps functionality
from another language.

00:10:11.790 --> 00:10:14.490
But why would you want to create
your own internal wrapper?

00:10:14.490 --> 00:10:17.760
Well, imagine a scenario
where a piece of functionality

00:10:17.760 --> 00:10:20.940
is available in one
language, but not the other.

00:10:20.940 --> 00:10:23.190
A well known example
is a statistical model

00:10:23.190 --> 00:10:26.850
that's released as an R package,
but not a Python package.

00:10:26.850 --> 00:10:28.230
But you want all
of your analysts

00:10:28.230 --> 00:10:32.310
to be able to access
that functionality.

00:10:32.310 --> 00:10:34.080
Another scenario
you might encounter

00:10:34.080 --> 00:10:36.450
is that both languages have
their own implementation

00:10:36.450 --> 00:10:39.510
of a piece of functionality,
like a statistical model.

00:10:39.510 --> 00:10:41.190
But maybe the
underlying computations

00:10:41.190 --> 00:10:43.040
aren't quite the same.

00:10:43.040 --> 00:10:47.480
And therefore your analysts
get different results.

00:10:47.480 --> 00:10:49.280
OK let's kick this
section off by looking

00:10:49.280 --> 00:10:52.630
at how we call Python from R.
There are many ways to do this.

00:10:52.630 --> 00:10:56.720
My method of choice is
the reticulate package.

00:10:56.720 --> 00:10:58.850
Reticulate gives you a
number of different ways

00:10:58.850 --> 00:11:01.770
to call Python code
in your R package.

00:11:01.770 --> 00:11:04.330
Let's look at a
couple of examples.

00:11:04.330 --> 00:11:06.740
Now, the first example is
importing Python modules

00:11:06.740 --> 00:11:08.820
into R. This works really well

00:11:08.820 --> 00:11:10.670
if the Python code
you're trying to access

00:11:10.670 --> 00:11:13.430
is contained within
a Python package.

00:11:13.430 --> 00:11:17.510
And to do this, I use the
import function from reticulate.

00:11:17.510 --> 00:11:19.290
I give it the name
of my module, here

00:11:19.290 --> 00:11:22.280
I call it py module, and
I assign it to a variable.

00:11:22.280 --> 00:11:26.390
And now I can access functions
from that module using

00:11:26.390 --> 00:11:27.715
this dollar sign notation.

00:11:30.240 --> 00:11:31.830
Now, a really good
tip for when you're

00:11:31.830 --> 00:11:35.280
doing this in an R package
is to create a global reference

00:11:35.280 --> 00:11:39.570
to your Python package
and then you can use it

00:11:39.570 --> 00:11:40.890
throughout your code.

00:11:40.890 --> 00:11:44.610
Notice here, I'm not actually
importing anything yet.

00:11:44.610 --> 00:11:47.430
I'm going to specify
how my Python module is

00:11:47.430 --> 00:11:49.710
imported in on load function.

00:11:49.710 --> 00:11:53.720
This will execute when
my R library is loaded.

00:11:53.720 --> 00:11:56.900
And here, I'm specifying
the delay load to true.

00:11:56.900 --> 00:11:59.870
And what this does is it
gives my end user the ability

00:11:59.870 --> 00:12:02.720
to specify their own custom
Python virtual environment

00:12:02.720 --> 00:12:05.980
to use with my package.

00:12:05.980 --> 00:12:09.220
Another way that you can use
Python code in your R package

00:12:09.220 --> 00:12:11.330
is to source Python files.

00:12:11.330 --> 00:12:13.990
This might work well if the
code you're trying to access

00:12:13.990 --> 00:12:16.720
is contained within
a Python script.

00:12:16.720 --> 00:12:20.930
To do this, I'm going to
use a Python file. Here,

00:12:20.930 --> 00:12:24.100
it's called add dot py, which
is contained in my R package

00:12:24.100 --> 00:12:28.630
and inside that file I define
a new function, add two. And now

00:12:28.630 --> 00:12:31.600
I will use source Python
from the reticulate package,

00:12:31.600 --> 00:12:34.500
and then I can access
the add two function.

00:12:37.210 --> 00:12:42.860
OK, we've called Python from R.
Now let's call R from Python.

00:12:42.860 --> 00:12:44.680
Again there are many
ways to do this.

00:12:44.680 --> 00:12:48.530
My method of choice is
the rpy2 package.

00:12:48.530 --> 00:12:50.870
rpy2 gives you a
number of different ways

00:12:50.870 --> 00:12:53.630
to call R code from
your Python package.

00:12:53.630 --> 00:12:56.820
Let's look at some examples.

00:12:56.820 --> 00:13:00.840
The first method is to import
R libraries into Python.

00:13:00.840 --> 00:13:03.120
To do this, we're going
to import the import

00:13:03.120 --> 00:13:05.760
R function from rpy2.

00:13:05.760 --> 00:13:07.560
And then in this
example, we're actually

00:13:07.560 --> 00:13:10.350
going to import
base R itself, which

00:13:10.350 --> 00:13:12.720
we can import like
any other library.

00:13:12.720 --> 00:13:14.710
To access a function
from base R,

00:13:14.710 --> 00:13:18.620
we use this notation. Here, I
am accessing the sum function.

00:13:21.182 --> 00:13:22.640
Now to use this
function, I'm going

00:13:22.640 --> 00:13:25.050
to have to do a
little bit of work.

00:13:25.050 --> 00:13:27.150
Sometimes when you're
using rpy2,

00:13:27.150 --> 00:13:30.960
you need to convert objects
between R and Python.

00:13:30.960 --> 00:13:34.140
In this example, I need
to convert my Python list

00:13:34.140 --> 00:13:36.330
into an R vector.

00:13:36.330 --> 00:13:40.230
I can do that using the int
vector function from rpy2.

00:13:40.230 --> 00:13:43.590
You can check out the rpy2
documentation for more tips

00:13:43.590 --> 00:13:46.570
on object conversion.

00:13:46.570 --> 00:13:50.680
Another way to access R
code from Python using rpy2

00:13:50.680 --> 00:13:53.740
is to create new
functions and access them

00:13:53.740 --> 00:13:55.370
from the R environment.

00:13:55.370 --> 00:13:57.520
This approach works
really well when

00:13:57.520 --> 00:13:59.620
you have multiple
computation steps that

00:13:59.620 --> 00:14:03.310
need to happen in
R. Now to do this,

00:14:03.310 --> 00:14:05.950
we're going to first
import the R objects

00:14:05.950 --> 00:14:08.710
module from rpy2.

00:14:08.710 --> 00:14:11.570
We're then going to define
our R code as a string.

00:14:11.570 --> 00:14:15.130
Here I'm just creating a new
function that returns one.

00:14:15.130 --> 00:14:18.430
I then execute that
R code using the R

00:14:18.430 --> 00:14:20.780
function from R objects.

00:14:20.780 --> 00:14:25.270
And then I can access that
new function using global env

00:14:25.270 --> 00:14:27.140
and assign it to a variable.

00:14:27.140 --> 00:14:31.450
And now Python is actually a
function that I can execute.

00:14:31.450 --> 00:14:33.400
Now a really great
utility that rpy2

00:14:33.400 --> 00:14:37.240
gives you is the ability to
define a temporary package

00:14:37.240 --> 00:14:38.740
structure.

00:14:38.740 --> 00:14:42.940
And we can do this by
importing the STAP function

00:14:42.940 --> 00:14:45.010
from rpy2.

00:14:45.010 --> 00:14:49.480
And just like before, we
store our R code as a string,

00:14:49.480 --> 00:14:54.730
and then we use STAP
to execute that code

00:14:54.730 --> 00:14:58.705
and then we can access functions
from that code using this dot

00:14:58.705 --> 00:14:59.205
notation.

00:15:02.310 --> 00:15:03.930
So this approach
works really well

00:15:03.930 --> 00:15:06.570
when you are
concerned about issues

00:15:06.570 --> 00:15:10.470
like namespace conflicts.

00:15:10.470 --> 00:15:12.930
OK now for the bad news.

00:15:12.930 --> 00:15:15.270
Cross-language wrappers have
their own technical quirks

00:15:15.270 --> 00:15:17.100
and headaches.

00:15:17.100 --> 00:15:18.900
For example, you
have twice the number

00:15:18.900 --> 00:15:20.410
of environments to think about.

00:15:20.410 --> 00:15:23.760
Your users will need to have
access to both Python and R,

00:15:23.760 --> 00:15:26.640
and your packages
will need to deal

00:15:26.640 --> 00:15:28.920
with the relevant dependencies.

00:15:28.920 --> 00:15:30.900
From personal
experience, reticulate

00:15:30.900 --> 00:15:34.230
is a lot more explicit about how
it handles Python environments,

00:15:34.230 --> 00:15:37.800
whereas with rpy2, you
need to do a little bit more

00:15:37.800 --> 00:15:41.230
of the work yourself.

00:15:41.230 --> 00:15:43.720
Now another issue is
object conversion.

00:15:43.720 --> 00:15:46.119
Sometimes to work with
cross-language wrappers,

00:15:46.119 --> 00:15:48.070
you need to handle
the conversion

00:15:48.070 --> 00:15:50.110
of an object from one
language to another,

00:15:50.110 --> 00:15:53.670
like you saw with rpy2.

00:15:53.670 --> 00:15:56.610
And a third issue
are error messages.

00:15:56.610 --> 00:15:59.490
Sometimes when an error
occurs in the language that

00:15:59.490 --> 00:16:01.500
is being wrapped,
the error message

00:16:01.500 --> 00:16:04.200
doesn't come through
in its entirety, which

00:16:04.200 --> 00:16:07.950
can frustrate your efforts
to create nice, helpful error

00:16:07.950 --> 00:16:09.060
messages.

00:16:09.060 --> 00:16:12.060
But with all this being
said, cross-language wrappers

00:16:12.060 --> 00:16:14.950
are a really useful tool
in your internal toolkit.

00:16:14.950 --> 00:16:16.890
They can deal with
issues of missing

00:16:16.890 --> 00:16:22.830
functionality or inconsistent
outputs between languages.

00:16:22.830 --> 00:16:25.350
And there we have it,
three different ways

00:16:25.350 --> 00:16:28.980
to build bilingual tools without
having to design two entirely

00:16:28.980 --> 00:16:30.960
different solutions.

00:16:30.960 --> 00:16:33.030
The goal of each
of these ideas is

00:16:33.030 --> 00:16:35.940
to make the development process
and the maintenance process

00:16:35.940 --> 00:16:38.520
much easier for you.

00:16:38.520 --> 00:16:40.890
If you're an internal
tool developer working

00:16:40.890 --> 00:16:43.260
in a bilingual
organization, I would

00:16:43.260 --> 00:16:47.100
encourage you to seek out
these programming concepts

00:16:47.100 --> 00:16:50.460
and ideas that can help to
bridge the gap between Python

00:16:50.460 --> 00:16:54.320
and R. Ultimately
the goal of this

00:16:54.320 --> 00:16:59.670
is to make your life as a
developer much, much easier.

00:16:59.670 --> 00:17:03.950
Thank you for listening
and happy developing.

